The Piddlington bank is quiet today, there is only the loans department open. A pot-plant sits quietly in the corner minding it's own business while a fly buzzes at the electric light. Also here is a cardboard leaflet holder advertising the bank's speciality deals.  From here, you can proceed east to the street outside or north to the loans 'department.' Mr Fairbanks' office now appears accessible.\par
\cf1 \par
w\par
\cf2 You move west.\par
Mr Fairbank appears to be the manager of the bank. He is sitting at a desk. There are pictures on the wall behind him.  The only way out of the office is to go east to the main room of the bank.\par
\cf1 \par
converse with mr fairbanks\par
\cf2 You tell Mr Fairbanks about your money problems. He appears marginally sympathetic but refuses to lend you any money. To try and get him to see your point of view you ask him about the pictures on the wall. He says that he is a member of the local wine-tasting club and has been lucky enough in the past to taste some amazing vintages. You ask him how much a bottle of a vintage wine can fetch. He replies that it is all dependant on the year, the vinter and the condition that the wine is in. You once again turn the conversation in the direction of money but he flatly refuses to lend you money in your current credit situation. You bitterly end the conversation.\par
\cf1 \par
give vintage to mr fairbanks\par
\cf2 You watch Mr Fairbanks' face light up as you show him the vintage. He snatches it off you like an excited child. He asks you where you got it from. You don't tell him, but instead ask him how much he would buy it off you for. He looks at you shrewdly and asks you to name your price. You start high and the two of you start haggling in earnest. In the end you settle for a thousand which he hands over to you quickly. You stand there with the grand in your hand.\par
